<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isthmus of Tehuantepec</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Isthmus of Tehuantepec is the narrowest part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, about 200 kilometres wide, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> in the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>. Long a corridor for trade between central Mexico and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, it was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a> of Zaachila around 1490. In modern times it has been crossed by an interoceanic railway, first opened in 1907.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-tehuantepec.jpg" alt="Isthmus of Tehuantepec" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kbh3rd · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isthmus_of_Tehuantepec-aeac.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The isthmus lies in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, with small areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>. At its narrowest it is 200 km across from gulf to gulf. The Sierra Madre del Sur breaks down there into a broad ridge, and the highest point reached by the railway, at Chivela Pass, is 224 m. The northern side is swampy and densely covered with jungle, which has been a greater obstacle to railway builders than the grades over the mountains. The name comes from the town of Santo Domingo Tehuantepec, from the Nahuatl Tecuantepec, meaning jaguar mountain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Since the conquest the Spanish monarchy sought a passage between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> and the Pacific. In 1520 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> used the isthmus to move equipment and supplies from the gulf to his shipyard on the Pacific coast, going part of the way by river. Later in the 16th century materials and artillery for shipbuilding were taken up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coatzacoalcos-river">Coatzacoalcos River</a> in canoes and carried on the backs of indigenous people to the Pacific. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> route eventually eclipsed Tehuantepec as the favoured site for a canal.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadsden-purchase">Gadsden Purchase</a> treaty of 1854 allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to carry mail and trade goods across the isthmus. The McLane-Ocampo Treaty of 1859, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> but never ratified by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a>, would have given extensive transit rights. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> contractors S. Pearson and Son began work on 10 December 1899 to rebuild the railway and construct ports at Coatzacoalcos and Salina Cruz, and the line was ready for formal opening in January 1907.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Tehuantepec railway, now the Ferrocarril Transístmico, is 308 km long and runs from Coatzacoalcos on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> to Salina Cruz on the Pacific. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a> half of the isthmus the people are mostly Zapotec, and the women known as Tehuanas are noted throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> for their colourful dress. The Selva Zoque in the east-central part is the largest remaining area of tropical rainforest in Mexico.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isthmus of Tehuantepec” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…called themselves is unknown, and so is their language, though in 1976 the linguists Lyle Campbell and Terrence Kaufman argued from early loanwords in other Mesoamerican languages that it was probably an ancestor of the Mixe–Zoquean languages, which are still spoken in and around the <b>Isthmus of Tehuantepec</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/olmecs">Olmecs · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Their languages, of which there are 7 distinct ones and over 100 dialects, are tone languages spoken in the Sierra Norte, the Central Valleys, the Southern Sierra, the <b>Isthmus of Tehuantepec</b> and along the Pacific coast, and through migration also in Mexico City and Los Angeles.</blockquote><a href="/culture/zapotec-civilisation">Zapotec civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1941 the composer Andres Henestrosa mentioned hearing it in the <b>Isthmus of Tehuantepec</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-llorona">La Llorona</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 15th century the Zapotec king Cocijoeza of Zaachila conquered the <b>Isthmus of Tehuantepec</b> from Mixe, Zoque and Huave peoples and moved his royal seat there, around 1490, gaining control of the trade route between central Mexico and Soconusco.</blockquote><a href="/state/zapotec-kingdom-of-tehuantepec">Zapotec Kingdom of Tehuantepec</a></li></ul></section>
